  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "section": "narrative",
      "text": "Two Halifax bombers of 78 Squadron, BB373 and JB874, took part in the Berlin raid on the night of 23/24 August 1943. Both had bombed and were coming back to Yorkshire when they were told to land away from base. The researcher's page says they were sent first to Leconfield and then on to Hutton Cranswick; the accident record card, quoted by CWGC, says they were diverted from Breighton because of poor weather. At about 04:30 they collided over Hull Bridge at Tickton, about two miles north-east of Beverley. The memorial inscription gives fog as the cause.",
      "cites": [
        "C2",
        "C5",
        "C4",
        "C8",
        "C6",
        "C10",
        "C9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section": "narrative",
      "text": "All eight men aboard BB373 died. They were pilot George Bell, second pilot Ronald Gilbert, engineer Albert Lester, navigator Edward Platt, bomb aimer William Clarke, wireless operator and gunner Arthur Lee, and air gunners David Walton and Robert Fletcher. Bell was a New Zealander and Walton an Australian; both are buried at Harrogate. Six of the seven aboard JB874 died: pilot Charles Keirl, engineer Ernest Scorey, navigator Harry Baylis, bomb aimer Henry Raymond Jones, wireless operator Charles Hilton and gunner Frederick Roberts. The seventh man, Sgt J. Greet, was seriously injured and spent about five years recovering. The researcher notes that 78 Squadron lost five aircraft and 31 airmen that night.",
